PROMETHEUS AND GRAFANA INSTALLATION ON KUBERNETES
================================================

Goal
----
Install Prometheus and Grafana on an existing Kubernetes cluster using Helm.
This guide uses kube-prometheus-stack, which installs Prometheus, Grafana,
Alertmanager, and Kubernetes monitoring components together.

IMPORTANT
---------
- Run these commands on a machine where kubectl already works for your cluster.
- The cluster must have enough CPU and memory for the monitoring components.
- Commands below assume a Linux control-plane/admin machine and a namespace
  named "monitoring".
- Do not expose dashboards publicly to everyone. Restrict access using your
  cloud firewall/security group or use a private network/VPN.

STEP 1 — Check that Kubernetes is working
-----------------------------------------
kubectl get nodes
kubectl get pods -A

Wait until your nodes are Ready before continuing.

STEP 2 — Check whether Helm is installed
----------------------------------------
helm version

If this displays a Helm version, continue to Step 3.

If "helm: command not found" appears, install Helm on Ubuntu/Debian:

sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y curl
curl -fsSL -o get_helm.sh https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/main/scripts/get-helm-3
chmod 700 get_helm.sh
./get_helm.sh
helm version

For another Linux distribution, follow the official Helm installation guide:

https://helm.sh/docs/intro/install/


STEP 3 — Add the Prometheus Community Helm repository
-----------------------------------------------------
helm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts
helm repo update

STEP 4 — Create the monitoring namespace
----------------------------------------
kubectl create namespace monitoring

If it already exists, Kubernetes may say it already exists; that is okay.
Confirm with:

kubectl get namespace monitoring


STEP 5 — Install Prometheus and Grafana
---------------------------------------
helm install monitoring prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack \
  --namespace monitoring

Wait for the installation to finish, then check:
helm list -n monitoring

kubectl get pods -n monitoring

Wait until the monitoring Pods are Running/Ready:

kubectl get pods -n monitoring -w

Press Ctrl+C after the Pods are ready.


STEP 6 — Check the installed Services
-------------------------------------

kubectl get svc -n monitoring

Look for Services with names similar to:
- monitoring-grafana
- monitoring-kube-prometheus-prometheus
- monitoring-kube-prometheus-operator

Exact names can vary by Helm release/chart version.

STEP 7 — Get the Grafana login credentials
------------------------------------------
Username is usually:
admin

Get the password from the Kubernetes Secret:
kubectl get secret monitoring-grafana -n monitoring -o jsonpath='{.data.admin-password}' | base64 -d
echo

If that Secret name does not exist, list the Grafana Secrets:
kubectl get secrets -n monitoring | grep grafana

Use the matching Secret name in the command above.

STEP 8 — Access Grafana without kubectl port-forward (NodePort)
---------------------------------------------------------------
First, change the Grafana Service to NodePort:
kubectl patch svc monitoring-grafana -n monitoring -p '{"spec":{"type":"NodePort"}}'

Find the actual assigned NodePort:
kubectl get svc monitoring-grafana -n monitoring

Example output might show:
80:32000/TCP

In this example, 32000 is the NodePort. YOUR PORT MAY BE DIFFERENT.
Use the port shown by your own command.

Find the public IP address of a Kubernetes node in your cloud console.
Then allow that NodePort in the node's cloud security group/firewall, preferably
only from your own public IP address. Do not open it to 0.0.0.0/0 unless you
fully understand and have secured the exposure.

Open in your browser:
http://NODE-PUBLIC-IP:NODEPORT

Replace NODE-PUBLIC-IP and NODEPORT with your actual values.
If nodes have no public IP or no public route, use a private network/VPN or a
properly secured ingress/load balancer instead.

STEP 9 — Access Prometheus without kubectl port-forward (optional NodePort)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Change the Prometheus Service to NodePort:
kubectl patch svc monitoring-kube-prometheus-prometheus -n monitoring -p '{"spec":{"type":"NodePort"}}'

Find the actual port:
kubectl get svc monitoring-kube-prometheus-prometheus -n monitoring

Example output might show:
9090:32100/TCP

YOUR PORT MAY BE DIFFERENT. Allow that port in the cloud security group/firewall
only from your own IP address, then open:
http://NODE-PUBLIC-IP:NODEPORT

STEP 10 — Confirm Grafana can use Prometheus
--------------------------------------------
In Grafana:
1. Log in with username admin and the password from Step 7.
2. Open Connections -> Data sources.
3. Open the Prometheus data source.
4. Use Save & test if shown.

The kube-prometheus-stack chart normally configures the Prometheus data source
automatically.

STEP 11 — Open a Kubernetes dashboard
-------------------------------------
In Grafana:
1. Open Dashboards -> Browse.
2. Search for dashboards beginning with "Kubernetes".
3. Try "Kubernetes / Compute Resources / Pod" if it is present.
4. Choose the namespace and Pod from the dashboard dropdowns.

The chart may install dashboards with slightly different names depending on
the version. You can also use Explore to query metrics directly.

STEP 12 — Try these Prometheus queries in Grafana Explore
---------------------------------------------------------
Open Explore and choose the Prometheus data source.

Pod information:
kube_pod_info

Pod status:
kube_pod_status_phase

Container memory:
container_memory_working_set_bytes

CPU usage rate:
rate(container_cpu_usage_seconds_total[5m])

To filter a specific Pod, replace mypod and default with your actual Pod name
and namespace:
kube_pod_info{namespace="default",pod="mypod"}

STEP 13 — Troubleshooting commands
----------------------------------
Check monitoring Pods:
kubectl get pods -n monitoring

Check Services and NodePorts:
kubectl get svc -n monitoring

Check Helm release:
helm list -n monitoring

Inspect Grafana Pod logs (replace POD_NAME with the actual Grafana Pod name):
kubectl logs -n monitoring POD_NAME

Describe a Pod if it is not starting (replace POD_NAME):
kubectl describe pod -n monitoring POD_NAME

If a dashboard is empty:
- Confirm the Prometheus data source is healthy.
- Confirm monitoring Pods are Running/Ready.
- Confirm you selected the correct namespace and Pod.
- Run "kubectl get pods -A" to find the real Pod name.
- Some metrics may take a few minutes to appear.

UNINSTALL (only if you intentionally want to remove the stack)
-------------------------------------------------------------
helm uninstall monitoring -n monitoring

This removes the Helm release, but the namespace may remain. Be careful:
removing monitoring can stop your monitoring dashboards and metrics collection.

